<h2> What is a linear equation? </h2>
<hr class='topic-header'>

<!-- def of linear eqn -->
<div class='def'>
    <p>A <strong>linear equation</strong> is any equation that describes a line.</p>

    <p>One way to describe a line is by identifying <span class='green-text'>where it crosses the y-axis</span> and <span class='orange-text'>how steep it is</span>.</p>

    <div id="linearEqnDef" class="jxgbox square-graph" <%- graphOpts %> ></div>

    <p>The <span class='green-text'>y-intercept</span> is the number on the y-axis where the line passes through. The <span class='orange-text'>slope</span> compares how fast the line travels to the right to how fast it travels up or down. </p>
</div>

<!-- eg of linear equation (in context) -->
<div class='eg'>
    <p><i>Example 1</i>. <strong> Omar is building a tree house. For every plank that he puts on, Omar is using four nails. Can we use a linear equation to represent <span class='underlined'>the relation between number of planks and number of nails</span>? </strong> </p>

    <p>We can create a graph or an equation to represent the linear equation. For this, we need to find the slope and the y-intercept.</p>

    <p> <strong>Part 1: Set up.</strong> </p>
    <p> We can describe the situation using a linear equation on a graph where the y-axis represents the number of nails and the x-axis represents the number of planks.</p>
    <div id="egLabels" class="jxgbox square-graph" <%- graphOpts %> ></div>
    <p>The equation will look like this, where y represents the number of nails and the x represents the number of planks.</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{\left[ \text{slope} \right]}x + \textcolor{#729170}{\left[ y\text{-intercept} \right]} $$

    <p><strong>Part 2: Find the y-intercept.</strong> </p>
    <p> The y-intercept is the value of y that corresponds to when x is 0. </p>
    <p>If x is 0, then there are no planks. How many nails would Omar need to put up 0 planks? The answer is none! Omar needs 0 nails to put up 0 planks. </p>
    <p> So, in this case, the y-value that corresponds to when x is 0 is also 0. That means that the linear equation crosses the y-axis at 0. We can plot the y-intercept in the graph. </p>
    <div id="egYint" class="jxgbox square-graph" <%- graphOpts %> ></div>
    <p>And we can substitute the y-intercept into the equation:</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{\left[ \text{slope} \right]}x + \textcolor{#729170}{\left[ y\text{-intercept} \right]} $$
    <p>Since the y-intercept is 0, the equation becomes:</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{\left[ \text{slope} \right]}x + \textcolor{#729170}{0} $$
    <p>The equation can be simplified as:</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{\left[ \text{slope} \right]}x $$

    <p><strong>Part 3: Find the slope.</strong> </p>
    <p> As we move to the right of the graph, we are increasing the number of planks. We are told that for every plank, Omar is using 4 nails. Therefore, every time we <span class='underlined'>move 1 unit to the right</span>, the line <span class='underlined'>climbs up 4 units</span>.</p>
    <div id="egSlope" class="jxgbox square-graph" <%- graphOpts %> ></div>
    <p>The slope is defined as how much we moved vertically over how much we moved horizontally.</p>
    $$\text{slope} = \frac{\text{vertical travel}}{\text{horizontal travel}} = \frac{4}{1} = 4 $$
    <p>So the slope of the linear equation is 4. Finally, we can substitute the slope into the equation:</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{\left[ \text{slope} \right]}x $$
    <p>After substituting, we have:</p>
    $$ y = \textcolor{#DA8359}{4}x $$

    <p><strong>Part 4: The linear equation.</strong></p>
    <p>In conclusion, we have the following linear graph to describe the relation of the number of planks that Omar is using for this tree house and the number of nails he uses.</p>
    <div id="egLine" class="jxgbox square-graph" <%- graphOpts %> ></div>
    <p>The same relation can be described by the following linear equation:</p>
    $$ \textcolor{#729170}{y = 4x} $$
</div>

